WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.420
I work hard to take care of

00:00:02.420 --> 00:00:06.680
my family. If I wasn't here, how

00:00:06.680 --> 00:00:08.360
would they manage without my income?

00:00:12.000 --> 00:00:14.680
Marcus works hard to support his family.

00:00:15.540 --> 00:00:18.460
His question gives us a practical way to learn the language

00:00:18.460 --> 00:00:22.340
of life insurance. By the end of this lesson, you should

00:00:22.340 --> 00:00:27.080
be able to separate risk, exposure, peril, hazard,

00:00:27.500 --> 00:00:31.660
and loss, and recognize the three common types of hazard.

00:00:32.580 --> 00:00:38.060
Start with risk. In insurance, risk means uncertainty about

00:00:38.060 --> 00:00:43.180
loss. Everyone eventually dies, but the timing is uncertain.

00:00:44.060 --> 00:00:47.100
Marcus does not know whether he will die while his children

00:00:47.100 --> 00:00:48.660
still depend on his income.

00:00:49.640 --> 00:00:51.440
That uncertainty is the risk.

00:00:52.160 --> 00:00:55.100
Insurance can help address the financial consequences.

00:00:56.280 --> 00:00:57.660
It does not prevent death.

00:00:58.290 --> 00:01:01.150
Exposure means being subject to a possible loss.

00:01:02.030 --> 00:01:04.950
Marcus's household depends on the support he provides.

00:01:05.810 --> 00:01:08.110
That dependence creates financial exposure.

00:01:09.130 --> 00:01:12.430
Think of exposure as what is vulnerable before anything happens.

00:01:13.230 --> 00:01:17.510
A household's expenses and responsibilities help an agent understand how much

00:01:17.510 --> 00:01:18.650
support could be needed.

00:01:19.610 --> 00:01:21.250
A peril is the cause of a loss.

00:01:22.110 --> 00:01:26.250
In this life insurance example, the peril is Marcus's death.

00:01:26.250 --> 00:01:30.330
The loss is the resulting harm, including the family's loss of

00:01:30.330 --> 00:01:32.210
his ongoing financial support.

00:01:32.770 --> 00:01:34.950
Keep the event separate from its consequences.

00:01:36.170 --> 00:01:37.170
Death is the event.

00:01:38.070 --> 00:01:39.930
Lost financial support is a consequence.

00:01:40.884 --> 00:01:43.784
A life insurance death benefit is a payment determined by the

00:01:43.784 --> 00:01:48.304
policy contract. It is not automatically equal to every dollar a

00:01:48.304 --> 00:01:52.574
family might lose. For example, a covered policy with a five

00:01:52.574 --> 00:01:56.704
hundred thousand dollar death benefit does not promise unlimited payments for

00:01:56.704 --> 00:01:58.444
every future household expense.

00:01:59.364 --> 00:02:01.104
The contract controls the benefit.

00:02:02.304 --> 00:02:06.184
Now add hazard. A hazard is a condition that increases the

00:02:06.184 --> 00:02:07.924
likelihood or severity of loss.

00:02:08.744 --> 00:02:11.304
Imagine defective safety equipment at a workplace.

00:02:12.324 --> 00:02:15.304
The equipment is a hazard before any accident occurs.

00:02:16.364 --> 00:02:19.724
It is a condition that increases danger, not the loss itself.

00:02:20.824 --> 00:02:24.224
This is a physical hazard because it is a tangible condition.

00:02:25.182 --> 00:02:27.062
Moral hazard involves dishonesty.

00:02:27.922 --> 00:02:31.262
An example is intentionally falsifying documents to obtain an insurance

00:02:31.262 --> 00:02:34.542
payment. The important clue is deliberate deception.

00:02:35.742 --> 00:02:38.902
An accidental paperwork mistake is different from deliberate deception.

00:02:40.022 --> 00:02:41.162
Morale hazard is different.

00:02:42.162 --> 00:02:45.502
Imagine someone ignoring safety precautions because they believe insurance

00:02:45.502 --> 00:02:47.182
will take care of any loss.

00:02:48.082 --> 00:02:52.302
That is carelessness or indifference associated with having insurance.

00:02:53.202 --> 00:02:58.242
Remember the contrast: moral hazard points to dishonesty; morale

00:02:58.242 --> 00:03:00.802
hazard points to a careless attitude.

00:03:01.602 --> 00:03:04.322
Read the behavior described in the question carefully.

00:03:07.750 --> 00:03:12.620
One more distinction is pure risk versus speculative risk.

00:03:13.360 --> 00:03:17.780
Pure risk involves a possibility of loss or no loss, with

00:03:17.780 --> 00:03:19.880
no opportunity for gain from the event.

00:03:20.980 --> 00:03:24.820
The possible loss of a parent's financial support is a pure

00:03:24.820 --> 00:03:29.120
risk. Speculative risk includes a chance of gain as well as

00:03:29.120 --> 00:03:33.360
loss. Buying a stock that could rise or fall is an

00:03:33.360 --> 00:03:38.400
example. Insurance generally deals with pure risks, but being

00:03:38.400 --> 00:03:42.260
a pure risk does not mean an exposure is automatically insurable.

00:03:43.170 --> 00:03:46.210
Return to Marcus. Uncertainty is risk.

00:03:47.070 --> 00:03:49.350
His family's vulnerability is exposure.

00:03:50.190 --> 00:03:51.190
Death is the peril.

00:03:52.050 --> 00:03:54.110
Lost financial support is the loss.

00:03:55.110 --> 00:03:57.470
A condition that increases danger is a hazard.

00:03:58.510 --> 00:04:02.370
When you face a practice question, identify exactly which part of

00:04:02.370 --> 00:04:03.630
that situation it describes.

00:04:04.590 --> 00:04:06.230
Next, answer the lesson questions.

00:04:07.210 --> 00:04:10.370
If you miss one, read the explanation and try it again.

00:04:10.370 --> 00:04:14.710
Use the explanations to understand the reason behind every answer.
